{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>The God of the Hebrews has called upon us.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the extra yod alludes to the ten plagues that He was destined to bring upon Egypt." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: they are called עברים (Hebrews) because they are destined to cross the sea. <b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the extra yod is to explain that the emanation is 'the God of the Hebrews,' for they are the children of Abraham, who is called in the Torah 'Abram the Hebrew' (אברם העברי), because he came from across the river; and this is that he first began with the unity of the Holy One, blessed be He. And this is what they said: the end is wedged in the beginning, like a flame joined to a coal. And so Solomon, peace be upon him, alluded (Ecclesiastes 12:1): ״וזכור את בוראיך״ — 'remember your Creator' — full with yod." "<b>A journey of three days.</b> Heaven forbid that this matter should be cunning in order to flee; rather, it was in order to receive the commandments. For the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to bring them into the commandments little by little, for they were first commanded concerning the Sabbath at Marah; and so we find in the case of Abraham, that He did not say to him immediately 'take please Isaac,' but rather (Genesis 22:2): ״קח נא את בנך את יחידך אשר אהבת את יצחק״ — 'Take, please, your son, your only one, whom you love, Isaac.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :register {:flags {0 ["God"], 1 ["God"]}}, :notes {1 "The Hebrew text opens with 'וע״ד המדרש' without the full phrase 'על דרך המדרש,' and similarly for the Kabbalah header; rendered with the fixed method-register in keeping with the voice rules. The word מלא ('full') refers to בוראיך being spelled with a yod."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:55.833261892Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}